udget} not in [{total_min},{total_max}]')\\n    current = list(minima)\\n    remaining = budget - total_min\\n    while remaining > 0:\\n        best = -1\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            if current[i] < capacities[i]:\\n                if best == -1:\\n                    best = i\\n                else:\\n                    left = importances[i] * (current[best] + 1)\\n                    right = importances[best] * (current[i] + 1)\\n                    if left > right:\\n                        best = i\\n                    elif left == right and i < best:\\n                        best = i\\n        if best == -1:\\n            raise ValueError('cannot allocate more, capacities exhausted')\\n        current[best] += 1\\n        remaining -= 1\\n    allocations = current\\n    total = sum(allocations)\\n    by_name = {names[i]: allocations[i] for i in range(n)}\\n    return {'allocations': allocations, 'by_name': by_name, 'total': total}\"}\n\nBut need to escape properly: newlines become \\\\n, backslashes inside code (like in f-string backslash? Actually the f-string uses braces, no backslashes. But we have double quotes inside the f-string? We have single quotes inside the string, that's fine. However the JSON string itself must have double quotes escaped. Our implementation string contains double quotes? In the return dict we used single quotes for keys 'allocations', 'by_name', 'total' – that's fine because they are Python string literals. But when we embed this as JSON string, we need to escape double quotes that appear in the code? The code itself doesn't contain double quotes unless we use them as string delimiters. We used single quotes everywhere. So no need to escape double quotes. But we have newlines and backslashes? The code has newline characters. In JSON string, newline must be escaped as \\\\n. So we replace each newline with two backslash n. Also any backslash in code? None. So we can do: take the code string, replace '\\n' with '\\\